hacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069304/assets/278088cea7543d913bd6e9702b03c58ff40e05413a543120087373c426bad83e.png) |\n\nSo, we obtain the following factorization:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069304/assets/b7457336851f79803231c1d2d64a07591ff7bb5e1f3315dd8af98bfe2782bcb4.png)\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069304/assets/6528f51e5d19fcda74b8ca490c4fd89b3e43269b57053da77b29284702c1f833.png) ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069304/assets/8c25f2182438cde814f01fd81ad6e94684ceed686db04002e7934b310e3261b1.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069304/assets/5e20e499636e1ce842b9d25a9934ce6956334c31f56d9cfd22f5230fbcbce983.png)" 13194139535672 true] [17592186124250 1145 "First, we find the matrix $\\, A-\\lambda I$:\n﻿﻿﻿$\\begin{bmatrix}1 - λ & -1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 - λ & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & -7 - λ\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe characteristic equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\det(A-\\lambda I) = 0\n$$\n\nExpanding the determinant across the $1$st row, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(A - λI) &= 0 \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}1 - λ & -1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 - λ & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & -7 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(1 - λ)\\begin{vmatrix}1 - λ & -2 \\\\ -2 & -7 - λ\\end{vmatrix}- (-1)\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -7 - λ\\end{vmatrix}+ (-2)\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 - λ \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(- λ^{3} - 5λ^{2} + 17λ - 11) + (3 + λ) - 2(4 - 2λ) &= 0 \\\\\n- λ^{3} - 5λ^{2} + 22λ - 16 &= 0 \\\\\nλ^{3} + 5λ^{2} - 22λ + 16 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to solve this equation. We find the first root by testing some values. According to the rational zeros theorem, a list of good first guesses consists of all divisors of $16$:\n$\\pm1, \\pm2, \\pm4, \\pm8, \\pm 16$\n\nLet's pick $x=1$. Substituting to the characteristic equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(1)^{3} + 5(1)^{2} - 22(1) + 16 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\lambda=1$ is a root of the equation. This means we can factor $(\\lambda-1)$ out of $\\lambda^3 +5 \\lambda^2 - 22 \\lambda + 16$ using synthetic division:\n\n| $1$ | $1$ | $5$ | $-22$ | $16$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | $\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$ | $1$ | $6$ | $-16$ |\n|  | $1$ | $6$ | $-16$ | $0$ |\n\nSo, we obtain the following factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(λ - 1)(λ^{2} + 6λ - 16) &= 0 \\\\\n(λ - 1)(λ - 2)(λ + 8) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=1\n$$\n\n$$\n\\,\\lambda_2=2,\\,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\,\\lambda_3=-8\n$$" 13194139535672 false] [17592186124298 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069304/assets/2379f9f0cec3f257da5a4c0695f114d0bc7f3b4c914edabfd690ff94705faceb.png)\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\lambda=4$ is an eigenvalue of $P$\n\n- $\\lambda=-1$ is an eigenvalue of $P$\n\n- The eigenvalue $\\lambda=6$ has algebraic multiplicity $2$" 13194139535672 true] [17592186124298 1100 "$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\lambda=4$ is an eigenvalue of $P$\n2. $\\lambda=-1$ is an eigenvalue of $P$\n3. The eigenvalue $\\lambda=6$ has algebraic multiplicity $2$" 13194139535672 false] [17592186124298 1145 "First, we find the matrix $\\, P-\\lambda I \\mathbin{:}$\n﻿﻿﻿![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069304/assets/8ee9ef991440e5641d6a458c0384382b54eb3d8504856b942fc33cf24b0b85e9.png)\n\nThe characteristic equation is $\\det(P-\\lambda I) = 0.$ \n\nNotice that we can expand the $4 \\times 4$ determinant across the $1$st column, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & 0 & -8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1-\\lambda  & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 4-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 7 & -3 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, expanding the $3 \\times 3$ determinant across the $2$nd row, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3-\\lambda )\\cdot (4-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ 7 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )((-1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-35) & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-36) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)(\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -6)(\\lambda +6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $P$ are $\\lambda=3,$ $\\lambda=4,$ $\\lambda=6,$ and $\\lambda=-6$ (all with algebraic multiplicity $1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535672 true] [17592186124298 1145 "First, we find the matrix $\\, P-\\lambda I$:\n﻿﻿﻿$\\begin{bmatrix}3 - λ & 0 & -8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 - λ & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 - λ & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 & 1 - λ\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\det(P-\\lambda I) = 0\n$$\n\nNotice that we can expand the $4 \\times 4$ determinant across the $1$st column, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(P - λI) &= 0 \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}3 - λ & 0 & -8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 - λ & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 - λ & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 & 1 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(3 - λ)\\begin{vmatrix}-1 - λ & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 4 - λ & 0 \\\\ 7 & -3 & 1 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, expanding the $3 \\times 3$ determinant across the $2$nd row, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(3 - λ) \\cdot (4 - λ)\\begin{vmatrix}-1 - λ & 5 \\\\ 7 & 1 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(3 - λ)(4 - λ)((-1 - λ)(1 - λ) - 35) &= 0 \\\\\n(3 - λ)(4 - λ)(λ^{2} - 36) &= 0 \\\\\n(λ - 3)(λ - 4)(λ - 